(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-alpha-central-vasodilators-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Alpha-1 Blockers, Central Agents & Vasodilators Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which pair of side effects is typical of central sympatholytics?","choices":["Cough and angioedema","Flushing and tachycardia","Sedation and dry mouth","Hair growth and edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drowsiness or sedation and dry mouth are central effects; sexual dysfunction, a narrow therapeutic range and abrupt withdrawal hypertension complete the list. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Hydralazine is N-acetylated in the liver. Which patients are at higher risk of its lupus syndrome?","choices":["Fast acetylators","Slow acetylators","Patients on low doses","Patients on short courses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Slow acetylators clear hydralazine more slowly; with high doses, long-term use, female sex and Caucasian ancestry, this raises the risk of lupus syndrome. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A patient takes clonidine together with a beta blocker. What happens to blood pressure if the clonidine is stopped abruptly?","choices":["It falls sharply","It stays the same","It rises sharply","It rises only slightly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. While both are taken they lower pressure together, but abrupt clonidine withdrawal in this combination produces a dramatic rise in blood pressure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"How does clonidine lower blood pressure?","choices":["Blocks central alpha-2 receptors","Blocks vascular alpha-1 receptors","Blocks renal beta-1 receptors","Stimulates central alpha-2 receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine is an alpha-2 and imidazoline agonist; stimulating central alpha-2A receptors inhibits sympathetic outflow and increases parasympathetic activity. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which drug is given by intravenous infusion for hypertensive crisis?","choices":["Nitroprusside","Hydralazine","Minoxidil","Clonidine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitroprusside is the hypertensive crisis agent, given by intravenous infusion; it dilates both veins and arterioles. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"When is oral minoxidil used for hypertension?","choices":["Hypertensive crisis by infusion","First line in young patients","Severe or refractory hypertension","Hypertension in pregnancy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Minoxidil is used in triple therapy, with a diuretic and a beta blocker, for severe or refractory hypertension. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which drug added to prazosin may enhance its postural hypotension?","choices":["An NSAID","A potassium supplement","A bile acid resin","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers may enhance prazosin's postural hypotension; NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) attenuate its response, and caution is needed in cardiac and renal failure. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A patient recently started on prazosin feels lightheaded every time he stands up. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Reflex bradycardia","First-dose hyperkalemia","Central sedation","Orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Orthostatic hypotension with postural dizziness is the leading prazosin side effect, alongside headache, drowsiness and lack of energy. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Why does tamsulosin have limited effects on blood pressure?","choices":["It does not reach the blood","It blocks beta-1 receptors too","It is alpha-1A selective","It is an alpha-2 agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tamsulosin selectively blocks alpha-1A receptors of the prostate, whereas vascular receptors are alpha-1B, so vascular effects are limited. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What effect does clonidine have on blood glucose?","choices":["It lowers it","It raises it","It masks lows only","It has no effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clonidine increases blood glucose by inhibiting insulin secretion. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"How does guanfacine compare with clonidine?","choices":["Less sedation","More potent","Less alpha-2 selective","More withdrawal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Guanfacine is less potent but the most selective (alpha-2 far more than alpha-1), causes less sedation, and has only an occasional withdrawal syndrome. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A patient on prazosin begins daily naproxen. What effect do NSAIDs (nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs) have on prazosin?","choices":["They enhance its hypotension","They attenuate its response","They raise its blood levels","They have no interaction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. NSAIDs attenuate the response to prazosin, as they also reduce the effect of ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"During a nitroprusside infusion, a patient develops trembling, vomiting and convulsions. Sodium thiosulfate is given. Which toxicity does it limit?","choices":["Cyanide toxicity","Thiocyanate toxicity","Lupus syndrome","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each nitroprusside molecule carries five cyanide groups; cyanide toxicity causes trembling, vomiting and convulsions, and sodium thiosulfate limits it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"How does minoxidil relax arteriolar smooth muscle?","choices":["Opens ATP-modulated potassium channels","Raises nitric oxide and cyclic GMP","Blocks L-type calcium channels","Blocks vascular alpha-1 receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased potassium efflux hyperpolarizes the cell and relaxes the muscle; its action on potassium channels also causes arrhythmias. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Hydralazine for chronic hypertension is combined with a beta blocker and a diuretic. What is the reason?","choices":["To prevent hydralazine lupus syndrome","To slow its hepatic acetylation","To block its cyanide metabolite","To offset reflex tachycardia and fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Direct vasodilators trigger reflex tachycardia, higher cardiac output, fluid retention and raised renin, which the beta blocker and diuretic counter. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Which patient is at greatest risk of hydralazine lupus syndrome?","choices":["A man on a short low-dose course","A woman on high doses long term","A fast acetylator on low doses","A child on a single dose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The risk factors are high dose, long-term therapy, female sex, slow acetylator status and Caucasian ancestry. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 101"}]};
